WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.220
One of the issues we haven't dove into too
deeply around here is gun control.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:13.300
The contentious, often venomous debate about
guns has been part of some of the conversations

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.650
we've had on The Rubin Report, especially
when talking about limited government, constitutional

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:22.570
authority, states rights and even freedom
of speech, but it has rarely been the focus

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:23.970
itself.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:29.090
Interestingly, gun control is one of the issues
I get the most emails about, with people usually

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:34.010
asking my personal feelings about the second
amendment and whether I believe in a right

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.350
to bear arms.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:39.760
You're not gonna believe this, but I think
there is a sane middle ground we can find

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.900
in the gun discussion that falls somewhere
between everyone should have a bazooka and

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.839
nobody should have a handgun.

00:00:46.839 --> 00:00:51.280
First off, very clearly and without reservation
I am a supporter of the 2nd amendment.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.659
The 2nd amendment specifically states, "A
well regulated Militia, being necessary to

00:00:56.659 --> 00:01:02.350
the security of a free State, the right of
the people to keep and bear Arms, shall not

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.250
be infringed."

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:09.350
This is interesting wording because it guarantees
the right of a citizen to protect themselves

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:14.950
from both other citizens and the state itself
by having the ability to own a gun.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:20.270
The guarantee to life, liberty and pursuit
of happiness which is enshrined in the Declaration

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.680
of Independence isn't only for the government
to ensure for you.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.140
Actually, it's quite the reverse.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:32.720
It is incumbent on you to protect your life,
liberty and pursuit of happiness, even from

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.970
the government if need be.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.540
In many ways this is the essence of individualism.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:43.150
While the state and the government are an
important facilitator of our rights, it ultimately

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:49.570
up to us, the individual to make sure we are
truly protected from people and governments

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.250
who would take those rights away.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.870
As I often say, however, two things can be
true at once.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:57.710
While having a populace that can defend itself
is important, at the same time there really

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:01.299
is little doubt to me that we do have a gun
problem in America.

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:05.040
Whether we're talking about school shootings
which happen all too often, or assassinations

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.830
of police officers as happened in Dallas last
year, there can be no doubt the gun is the

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:14.400
weapon of choice when someone wants to indiscriminately
kill as many people as possible.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.050
I should pause and separate mass shootings
from terrorism specifically, where terrorists

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:23.690
usually use some sort of explosive to kill
as many as possible, though recently we've

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.370
seen guns, knives and even cars and trucks
thrown into the mix.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:32.370
And it's important to remember that the weapon
itself doesn't make the distinction between

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:38.340
a criminal act or terrorism, the ideology
behind the act does.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.440
The fact that there are many ways to kill
people, does bring up an interesting idea

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:43.650
about guns.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.910
If I had an assault rifle or any other gun,
I wouldn't go into a school or a church or

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.319
anywhere else and kill randomly people.

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:55.420
Likewise, I've got some pretty great kitchen
knives and I don't go into mosques or temples

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:56.450
and stab anyone.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:01.780
This shows that the gun itself is just the
tool and the issue around gun control is much

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:02.800
deeper.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.410
We should be having an honest conversation
about issues like mental health, the overuse

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:13.209
and side effects of prescription drugs, the
insidious nature of evil ideologies, the access

00:03:13.209 --> 00:03:16.530
to guns that mentally unstable people have,
and much more.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:21.590
Unfortunately, we seem to only have these
conversations at the worst possible time,

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.300
right after another shooting.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:28.530
This gets the anti gun crowd in a frenzy to
take away all guns, and it gets the pro gun

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:32.840
crown to think the government is going to
come to their house and take their guns.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:33.840
Don't believe me?

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:41.150
Just check out how gun sales which skyrocketed
under Obama, but now have dipped under Trump.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.770
All of these issues are just the tip of the
iceberg when it comes to guns.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:50.069
I think there are good people on both sides
of the debate who just see a different solution,

00:03:50.069 --> 00:03:53.510
partly because they are seeing a different
problem in the first place.

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:58.680
Some see this truly as a problem with guns
themselves while some see this as a larger

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:05.040
societal problem with mental health, but perhaps
there is merit to both arguments.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.840
Joining me today to unpack this highly charged
discussion is Antonia Okafor.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:15.019
Antonia is a second amendment advocate, who
spends most of her energy pushing for legislation

00:04:15.019 --> 00:04:18.810
to allow woman to carry guns on college campuses.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:24.309
As a young, black conservative, she's found
herself on the outside of what the mainstream

00:04:24.309 --> 00:04:27.080
says someone who like her should believe.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.919
We'll talk about her political evolution from
a Obama voter to a Trump supporter and why

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:37.830
she thinks that a woman's right to defend
herself by herself is so important.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.159
Without the reactionary hysteria we are usually
caught in when discussing guns, can the two

00:04:42.159 --> 00:04:44.580
of us find some common ground?

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:45.319
Only one way to find out...

